  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  The migration moves the backend from JDK 8 to Java 17, and the frontend build toolchain to
  Node 20 LTS, while preserving observable behaviour exactly. R-5 requires that failing tests are
  never disabled and that any exclusion is limited to a failure already present at baseline, so a
  recorded baseline is the only thing that can ever justify an exclusion. This folder is that
  record for one Maven module: exactly 14 report files, one per unit test class, and nothing else.
  This class is one of the nine API controller reports in the folder, and that group carries a
  specific evidentiary burden. REST API contracts, meaning paths, verbs, request and response
  shapes and authentication behaviour, must survive the migration with no controller signature, no
  request mapping change and no serialised field change.

  PROVENANCE, STATED PLAINLY: SOURCE DERIVED STRUCTURAL INVENTORY, NOT A CAPTURED RUN
  A genuine baseline run could not be executed here, for three independent reasons:
    1. No JDK 8 is installed in this checkout; only the migration target runtime is present.
       Installing a runtime falls outside R-1's scope fence, which limits this work to POSIX text
       tooling that installs nothing, and a capture taken on the target runtime would in any case
       be a capture from the wrong runtime.
    2. The checkout already carries the migration change set, so the pre edit tree state that a
       baseline requires is no longer reachable without history altering git operations that are
       prohibited on this branch.
    3. A run on the runtime that is present would resolve the migrated dependency graph, and would
       therefore be a migrated capture wearing a baseline label.
  No runtime, no compiler and no build tool was installed to make the capture look complete.

  WHAT THIS FILE DOES AND DOES NOT ASSERT (R-T7)
  R-T7 requires validation to assert on produced artifacts and captured output rather than on
  process exit status alone, and by extension it forbids asserting an outcome that a run did not
  produce. Accordingly:
    * Every time attribute is the literal 0. That is a deliberate, documented "not measured"
      marker; it is not a claim of zero elapsed duration.
    * tests="3" is a source fact: the class declares exactly three methods annotated as tests.
      skipped="0" is a source fact: the class carries no ignore annotation, and neither does any
      other of the 14 unit test classes in this module.
    * failures="0" and errors="0" are structural. This inventory records no observed failure or
      error because nothing was observed at all. Equally, the absence of a failure or error child
      on a testcase below is not evidence that the test passed.
    * Nothing measured has been invented. There is no host attribute, no timestamp attribute, no
      JVM system property dump, no fabricated duration and no fabricated stack trace.
    * No row of the baseline test failures register, referred to here by title only, may cite this
      file. A register row requires a genuine capture. This file records which tests exist, not
      how they behaved.

  RULES PROVENANCE, BOTH FACTS STATED TOGETHER
    1. There is no on disk user rules document for this project. The rules facility was queried and
       reported plainly that no user rules were provided, so there is no external full text source
       to defer to. That absence is stated explicitly and is not licence to lower the bar:
       enterprise standard practice is applied on top of the rules below, never instead of them.
    2. Rules are nonetheless present and binding. The requirements embed a numbered block of seven
       rules, plus seven transformation rules, which govern this work in full exactly as an
       external rules document would. The identifiers R-1 through R-7 and R-T1 through R-T7 used
       here are the migration plan's own navigational convention, not quoted titles.

  R-7 AS IT BINDS THIS FILE, WITH ITS SUBSTITUTION DISCLOSED
  R-7 makes the application's observed behaviour at the base commit on JDK 8 the tie breaker for
  any ambiguity, and requires each resolution to be documented. Editorial note, disclosed here
  rather than made silently: the rule's own text names the older runtime with a phrase this
  documentation tree's wording gate forbids, so "JDK 8" is substituted above; the substitution
  changes no meaning.

